scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1055:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_438_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_438_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,"In an action by a lessor against an overholding lessee for restoration and ejectment, privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief, and issues as to title are irrelevant to the proceedings.",Privity of contract (whether it be by original agreement or by attornment) is the foundation of the right to relief and issues as to title are irrelevant to the proceedings.,SRC029-s58,section-not-found,section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1103:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party who claims prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.","A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1108:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf,,SC,2022,"A rei vindicatio action proper requires proof of title, but an action against an overholding lessee or licensee is based on privity of contract and issues as to title are irrelevant.",What has been discussed above indicate the deference between the re vidicatio action and an acton against an overholding lessee or licensee.,SRC029-s116,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1134:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proving that there had been a change in the condition of the land solely rests on the appellants.,Therefore the burden of proving that there had been a change in the condition of the land solely rests on the appellants.,SRC029-s101,section-not-found,Section 101 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1140:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party alleging that a deed is a forgery must prove the execution of the deed to establish its genuineness.,"If these deeds are excluded, the Plaintiff cannot establish his title and his action will necessarily fail.",SRC029-s114,section-not-found,section 114 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1158:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the person who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.,SRC029-s103,section-not-found,Section 103 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1169:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A Notary Public who attested a Deed cannot be regarded as an attesting witness for the purposes of Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if he does not know the executant of the Deed.,"Therefore, the fact that, the Notary Public did not state in his Attestation that he knew the executants, leads compellingly to the conclusion that, he did not know the executants.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1172:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A statutory presumption in favour of the execution of a judicial or official act can be made if it is likely to have happened, considering the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business.","This Court rules on a statutory presumption in favour of the execution, in terms of Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance. This Section reads as follows; 4 “The Court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.”",SRC029-s114(d),none,Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1178:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_163_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_163_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A document marked subject to proof is admissible in evidence if the opposing party does not object to it.,"This principle is applicable even to Deeds, irrespective of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. (Siyadoris v. Danoris (1841) 42 NLR 311, Silva v. Kindersly (1914) 18 NLR 85, Seyed Mohomed v. Perera (1956) 58 NLR 246, Cinemas Ltd v. Sounderarajan [1998] 2 Sri LR 16 at 18, Hemapala v. Abeyratne [1978-79] 2 Sri LR 222, Wijeardena v. Ellawala [1991] 2 Sri LR 14 at 34-35, Kandasamy v. Sinnathamby [1985] 2 Sri LR 249 at 255) What I have stated above shall not be taken to mean that all documents the opposing party purportedly requires to be marked subject to proof must necessarily be proved by calling witnesses.",,none,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1197:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1212:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,"A document marked subject to proof is admissible without formal proof unless its execution or genuineness is impeached in the pleadings and raised as an issue, or the court otherwise requires proof.","The law relating to the proof of documents has been laid down in the Civil Procedure Code (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2022. Section 2 of this Act introduces section 154A to be immediately inserted after section 154 of the principal enactment, which provides that when a deed or any document required by law to be attested is tendered in evidence, it shall be admissible without requiring formal proof, unless its execution or genuineness is impeached in the pleadings and raised as an issue, or the court otherwise requires proof.",SRC029-s154A,section-not-found,"Section 2 of the Civil Procedure Code (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2022",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1219:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The mere fact that Dingiri Banda is identified as a close relative of Siyathuhamy is insufficient by itself to make the information about his mother relevant in terms of section 32(5) of the Evidence Ordinance in view of the contradictory nature of the evidence before court on this issue.,"Therefore, I hold that the details of the mother of Siyathuhamy set out in the death certificate (4D4) are not relevant in terms of section 32(5) of the Evidence Ordinance.",,bad-source,section 32(5) of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1230:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Formal admissions recorded at the trial need no further proof unless the Court wants them to be proved.,According to section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance such formal admissions recorded at the trial need no further proof unless the Court wants them to be proved.,,none,section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1235:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A person who possesses an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1250:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_2_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_2_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"An appeal abates only against the deceased respondent, but not against the surviving respondents.","On the death of a respondent, an appeal abates only against the deceased, but not against the other surviving respondents;",,bad-source,Section 41A (2) of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1253:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1256:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A person cannot acquire prescriptive title without demonstrating an overt act showing an intention to possess adversely to the owner.,"Without any demonstration of any overt act against the rights of the owner, court cannot recognize Nandasekera as a person who has acted as one holding prescriptive possession against the owner who had given him leave and license to step in and live there, no matter how long he had enjoyed the property.",SRC029-s3,section-not-found,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1263:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A deed required by law to be attested must be proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if it is impeached or challenged through an issue raised.,"However, if the deed is impeached or challenged through an issue raised, it has to be proved as per the provisions of Evidence Ordinance.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1281:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit.,"A licencee as the Defendant who entered the property in dispute with the leave and licence of Plaintiff cannot in law challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit, in this case the Plaintiff party.",SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1283:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A document required to be attested cannot be used as evidence if it is not proved in accordance with the procedure laid down in section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance, even if it is not objected to at the close of the case.","A document cannot be used in evidence, unless its genuineness has been either admitted or established by proof, which should be given before the document is accepted by Court.",,none,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1296:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,"A party relying on a notarially executed deed to transfer immovable property must prove the document as required by law, specifically by calling an attesting witness to prove its execution, unless there is a written admission recorded at the trial.","Section 68; If a document is required by law to be attested, it should not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there be an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence",,none,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1305:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A court must consider all prayers in a plaint when deciding on an ex parte judgment.,"The learned District Judge has not stated, in his judgment, that the Plaintiff also seeks a declaration to the effect that deed No 1014 dated 29.12.1994 attested by IM Wimalasena is a valid deed.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1317:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"A court must issue notice on respondents when a prima facie case of an illegality is established, warranting full investigation with the participation of all parties.","In order to have notice issued on the Respondents, the burden cast on the Appellant was to establish a prima facie sustainable case and for the Court to be satisfied that there is a prima facie case to be looked into.",SRC029-s5,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1319:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A licensee of an owner of a property cannot acquire prescriptive title to the property against the owner of the property so long as he holds the status of a licensee.,I hold that licensee of an owner of a property cannot acquire prescriptive title to the property against the owner of the property so long as he holds the status of a licensee.,SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1320:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.,"However, in MMBL Teas (Pvt) Ltd. v. British Ceylon Produce Export (Pvt) Ltd. [S.C. Case No. SC/CHC/35/2008, S.C.M. 17.12.2021] Aluwihare, PC, J., (at page 17) was of the view that failure to challenge evidence by cross examination by itself may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance. It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s2,ok,Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1326:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The burden of proving that a property is Thediathetam is on the party who claims it to be so.,I am of the view that the burden of proving that a property is Thediathetam is on the party who claims it to be so;,SRC029-s19,section-not-found,Section 106 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1338:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1388:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party is not precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",,none,Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1393:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,A surety who has undertaken liability as co-principal debtor has impliedly and tacitly renounced their right to plead the defence of beneficium ordinis seu excussionis.,"I am therefore of the view that by undertaking liability as co-principal debtor, the Defendants have impliedly as well as tacitly renounced their right to plead the defence of beneficium ordinis seu excussionis.",SRC029-s101,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1410:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer's transfer must be based on a valid reason and the officer must be made aware of the reason.,"The PSC Rules recognise three different categories of exigencies of service, so merely stating that it was on exigencies of service is not sufficient.",SRC029-s114(f),none,Article 12 (1) of the Constitution,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:72:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-11-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-11,79 NLR 202,LKCA,1977,A power of attorney ex-facie duly executed in India is presumed to be duly executed and authenticated.,"That a power of attorney which is ex-facie duly executed in India attracts the presumption laid down in section 85 of the Evidence Ordinance that is was so duly executed and authenticated. Held further : That according to the provisions of the Indian Law applicable, a Sub-Registrar is a person duly authorised by law to execute and authenticate a document such as a Power of Attorney and therefore the document in the present case is ex facie duly executed",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:76:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,A misdescription of land boundaries in a deed does not invalidate the deed.,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:83:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-21-1,commonlii-LKCA-1980-21,(1980) 2 Sri LR 136,LKCA,1980,A plaintiff can plead multiple incidents in a single action if they are all part of the same cause of action.,The learned District Judge had misdirected himself in holding that the averments in the plaint constituted several causes of action which were embodied in one action. The plaint refers to one cause of action against all the defendant respondents though various incidents which had taken place between 1955 and 1963 were mentioned. On the facts pleaded and proved in the case there was no misjoinder of parties and causes of action. The title deed was properly proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. There was no issue as to whether the notary complied with the provisions of section 31(9) of the Notaries Ordinance and no questions had been asked from him regarding that when he gave evidence,,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:84:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-21-2,commonlii-LKCA-1980-21,(1980) 2 Sri LR 136,LKCA,1980,A notary's failure to disclose a conviction under the Notaries Ordinance may not be a ground for questioning his credibility.,The learned District Judge had misdirected himself in holding that the averments in the plaint constituted several causes of action which were embodied in one action. The plaint refers to one cause of action against all the defendant respondents though various incidents which had taken place between 1955 and 1963 were mentioned. On the facts pleaded and proved in the case there was no misjoinder of parties and causes of action. The title deed was properly proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. There was no issue as to whether the notary complied with the provisions of section 31(9) of the Notaries Ordinance and no questions had been asked from him regarding that when he gave evidence,,none,,,headnote,fact-specific,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:90:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-45-1,commonlii-LKCA-1980-45,(1980) 2 Sri LR 214,LKCA,1980,P4 is a more solemn document than D3.,"P4 is a more solemn document than D3 where the declarant was bound by statute to declare the truth. Sections 32(5), 32(6) and 50 of the Evidence Ordinance refer to relevance and admissibility and not to presumptions. But such evidence can always be countered by other evidence, as in this case, by evidence more convincing and cogent",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:91:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-45-2,commonlii-LKCA-1980-45,(1980) 2 Sri LR 214,LKCA,1980,"The Evidence Ordinance sections 48 and 50 refer to relevance and admissibility, not presumptions.","P4 is a more solemn document than D3 where the declarant was bound by statute to declare the truth. Sections 32(5), 32(6) and 50 of the Evidence Ordinance refer to relevance and admissibility and not to presumptions. But such evidence can always be countered by other evidence, as in this case, by evidence more convincing and cogent",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:98:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1983-13-1,commonlii-LKCA-1983-13,(1983) 2 Sri LR 407,LKCA,1983,An oral agreement to reconvey lands does not give rise to a trust.,"The oral agreement by the defendant appellant to reconvey the lands to the plaintiff -respondent on payment of Rs.3000/- does not give rise to a trust. Further facts, clearly indicative of a trust must be proved before a trust can arise. The facts that adequate consideration did not pass, that plaintiff -respondent remained in possession after execution of the conveyance and that an oral promise was made to re-transfer are insufficient. Further this oral agreement relied on by the plaintiff -respondent amounts to a contract for the transfer of immovable property which is invalid and cannot be enforced as it contravenes section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance. Though parol evidence of this oral agreement was admitted at the trial without objections it would still be prohibited by section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:130:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-34-1,commonlii-LKCA-1985-34,(1985) 2 Sri LR 249,LKCA,1985,The execution of a deed of gift can be established through secondary evidence.,"(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:131:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-34-2,commonlii-LKCA-1985-34,(1985) 2 Sri LR 249,LKCA,1985,Particulars in a Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance are admissible as secondary evidence.,"(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
