scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1905:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf,,SC,2020,Courts may countenance infirmities in proving original ownership of land if it is a realistic approach rather than a legalistic one.,"Courts by and large countenance infirmities in this regard, if infirmities they be, in an approach which is realistic rather than legalistic, as to do otherwise would be to put the relief given by partition decrees outside the reach of very many persons seeking to end the co-ownership.",SRC029-s32(5),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1924:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A judge must consider all prayers in a plaint, even in an ex parte trial.","Even in an ex parte trial, the judge must act according to law and ensure that the relief claimed is due in fact and in law, and must dismiss the plaintiff's claim if he is not entitled to it. An ex parte judgment cannot be entered without a hearing and an adjudication.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1926:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A party must prove the due execution of a document to establish its validity.,"The learned judge has noted that both the 2nd Defendant and the Plaintiff have not discharged the burden cast on them by section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance read with section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, which required them to prove that the two documents",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1941:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A court must consider the relevance of evidence before admitting it.,"Evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of the existence or non-existence of every fact in issue, and of such other facts as are hereinafter declared to be relevant and of no others.",SRC029-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1945:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1950:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.,"The judicial approach to authenticity was clearly articulated in the case of Robins v Grogan [43 N.L.R. 269 at 270], where the Court held that a document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.",SRC029-s67,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1979:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2012:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,The burden of proof lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.,Section 102 – “The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.”,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2043:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A tenant cannot deny the landlord's title to the property during the continuance of the tenancy.,"No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property;",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2054:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,Uncontradicted evidence adduced by a party is a fact in favour of that party's case.,"In other words, uncontradicted evidence adduced by the 2nd Defendant in the trial is a fact in favour of the 2nd Defendant’s case.",SRC029-s03,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2062:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A document cannot be produced in evidence without leave in terms of section 175(2) of the Civil Procedure Code if it was not included in a list filed in terms of section 121(2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,It must be noted here that a document cannot be produced in evidence without leave in terms of section 175(2) of the Civil Procedure Code if it was not included in a list filed in terms of section 121(2) of the Civil Procedure Code and that new matters cannot be introduced during re-examination without permission in terms of section 138(3) of the Evidence Ordinance.,,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2072:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A notary who attests a document in terms of Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is generally competent to testify under section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance.,A notary who attests a deed is an attesting witness within the meaning of that expression in section 68 and section 69 of the Evidence Ordinance.,SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2083:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party may lead evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2092:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.","A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.",SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2120:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A document required by law to be attested must have at least one attesting witness called to prove its execution.,"According to that section, such document shall not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there is an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2151:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.,"Moreover, a public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.",SRC029-s114(f),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:72:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-11-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-11,79 NLR 202,LKCA,1977,A power of attorney ex-facie duly executed in India is presumed to be duly executed and authenticated.,"That a power of attorney which is ex-facie duly executed in India attracts the presumption laid down in section 85 of the Evidence Ordinance that is was so duly executed and authenticated. Held further : That according to the provisions of the Indian Law applicable, a Sub-Registrar is a person duly authorised by law to execute and authenticate a document such as a Power of Attorney and therefore the document in the present case is ex facie duly executed",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:76:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,A misdescription of land boundaries in a deed does not invalidate the deed.,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:83:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-21-1,commonlii-LKCA-1980-21,(1980) 2 Sri LR 136,LKCA,1980,A plaintiff can plead multiple incidents in a single action if they are all part of the same cause of action.,The learned District Judge had misdirected himself in holding that the averments in the plaint constituted several causes of action which were embodied in one action. The plaint refers to one cause of action against all the defendant respondents though various incidents which had taken place between 1955 and 1963 were mentioned. On the facts pleaded and proved in the case there was no misjoinder of parties and causes of action. The title deed was properly proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. There was no issue as to whether the notary complied with the provisions of section 31(9) of the Notaries Ordinance and no questions had been asked from him regarding that when he gave evidence,,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:84:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-21-2,commonlii-LKCA-1980-21,(1980) 2 Sri LR 136,LKCA,1980,A notary's failure to disclose a conviction under the Notaries Ordinance may not be a ground for questioning his credibility.,The learned District Judge had misdirected himself in holding that the averments in the plaint constituted several causes of action which were embodied in one action. The plaint refers to one cause of action against all the defendant respondents though various incidents which had taken place between 1955 and 1963 were mentioned. On the facts pleaded and proved in the case there was no misjoinder of parties and causes of action. The title deed was properly proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. There was no issue as to whether the notary complied with the provisions of section 31(9) of the Notaries Ordinance and no questions had been asked from him regarding that when he gave evidence,,none,,,headnote,fact-specific,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:90:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-45-1,commonlii-LKCA-1980-45,(1980) 2 Sri LR 214,LKCA,1980,P4 is a more solemn document than D3.,"P4 is a more solemn document than D3 where the declarant was bound by statute to declare the truth. Sections 32(5), 32(6) and 50 of the Evidence Ordinance refer to relevance and admissibility and not to presumptions. But such evidence can always be countered by other evidence, as in this case, by evidence more convincing and cogent",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:91:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1980-45-2,commonlii-LKCA-1980-45,(1980) 2 Sri LR 214,LKCA,1980,"The Evidence Ordinance sections 48 and 50 refer to relevance and admissibility, not presumptions.","P4 is a more solemn document than D3 where the declarant was bound by statute to declare the truth. Sections 32(5), 32(6) and 50 of the Evidence Ordinance refer to relevance and admissibility and not to presumptions. But such evidence can always be countered by other evidence, as in this case, by evidence more convincing and cogent",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:98:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1983-13-1,commonlii-LKCA-1983-13,(1983) 2 Sri LR 407,LKCA,1983,An oral agreement to reconvey lands does not give rise to a trust.,"The oral agreement by the defendant appellant to reconvey the lands to the plaintiff -respondent on payment of Rs.3000/- does not give rise to a trust. Further facts, clearly indicative of a trust must be proved before a trust can arise. The facts that adequate consideration did not pass, that plaintiff -respondent remained in possession after execution of the conveyance and that an oral promise was made to re-transfer are insufficient. Further this oral agreement relied on by the plaintiff -respondent amounts to a contract for the transfer of immovable property which is invalid and cannot be enforced as it contravenes section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance. Though parol evidence of this oral agreement was admitted at the trial without objections it would still be prohibited by section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:130:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-34-1,commonlii-LKCA-1985-34,(1985) 2 Sri LR 249,LKCA,1985,The execution of a deed of gift can be established through secondary evidence.,"(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:131:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-34-2,commonlii-LKCA-1985-34,(1985) 2 Sri LR 249,LKCA,1985,Particulars in a Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance are admissible as secondary evidence.,"(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:132:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-34-3,commonlii-LKCA-1985-34,(1985) 2 Sri LR 249,LKCA,1985,A deed of gift prevails over another deed if it was executed first.,"(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:179:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1987-30-1,commonlii-LKCA-1987-30,(1987) 1 Sri LR 242,LKCA,1987,A notarially executed deed cannot be varied to include characteristics such as mortgages or agreements to retransfer through evidence.,Apart from the fact that Ss.91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not permit the receipt of evidence to vary the terms of a notarially executed deed so as to superimpose on a simple transfer deed characteristics such as mortgages or agreements to retransfer yet even on the facts no trust can be held to have been established. Time was of the essence of the alleged oral agreement and the constructive trust yet there was no evidence that the money was even tendered in time,,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:345:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-50-1,commonlii-LKCA-1998-50,(1999) 3 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1998,A donor under the Kandyan Law retains the right to revoke a gift during their lifetime without the consent of the donee or any other person.,"1. The Kandyan Law reserves to the donor the right to revoke a gift during his life- time and without the consent of the donee or any other person and therefore it is not open for the donee acting unilaterally to deny the donor a right that is reserved under s. 4 (1), and s. 5 (1) and provides for the renunciation of the right to revoke, which right should be expressly renounced by the donor, either in the same deed or by any subsequent instrument. 2 2. S. 4 (1) and s. 5 (1) read together clearly spell out the donors right to revoke, and the donee by a subsequent retransfer to a 3rd party could not defeat the donors right to revoke a gift during his lifetime and without the consent of the donee or any other person. 3. The execution of the deed of revocation was not challenged and not put in issue at the trial. S. 68 of the Evidence Ordinance prohibits the use as evidence of any document required by law to be attested until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution. 4. Once a gift becomes void after revocation in terms of s. 4 (1) registration of other deeds or registration in the proper folio will not revive a deed that is void",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:346:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-50-2,commonlii-LKCA-1998-50,(1999) 3 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1998,A deed of gift must expressly renounce the right to revoke.,"1. The Kandyan Law reserves to the donor the right to revoke a gift during his life- time and without the consent of the donee or any other person and therefore it is not open for the donee acting unilaterally to deny the donor a right that is reserved under s. 4 (1), and s. 5 (1) and provides for the renunciation of the right to revoke, which right should be expressly renounced by the donor, either in the same deed or by any subsequent instrument. 2 2. S. 4 (1) and s. 5 (1) read together clearly spell out the donors right to revoke, and the donee by a subsequent retransfer to a 3rd party could not defeat the donors right to revoke a gift during his lifetime and without the consent of the donee or any other person. 3. The execution of the deed of revocation was not challenged and not put in issue at the trial. S. 68 of the Evidence Ordinance prohibits the use as evidence of any document required by law to be attested until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution. 4. Once a gift becomes void after revocation in terms of s. 4 (1) registration of other deeds or registration in the proper folio will not revive a deed that is void",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:347:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-50-3,commonlii-LKCA-1998-50,(1999) 3 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1998,A donor's right to revoke a gift during their lifetime cannot be defeated by a subsequent retransfer to a third party without the consent of the donee or any other person.,"1. The Kandyan Law reserves to the donor the right to revoke a gift during his life- time and without the consent of the donee or any other person and therefore it is not open for the donee acting unilaterally to deny the donor a right that is reserved under s. 4 (1), and s. 5 (1) and provides for the renunciation of the right to revoke, which right should be expressly renounced by the donor, either in the same deed or by any subsequent instrument. 2 2. S. 4 (1) and s. 5 (1) read together clearly spell out the donors right to revoke, and the donee by a subsequent retransfer to a 3rd party could not defeat the donors right to revoke a gift during his lifetime and without the consent of the donee or any other person. 3. The execution of the deed of revocation was not challenged and not put in issue at the trial. S. 68 of the Evidence Ordinance prohibits the use as evidence of any document required by law to be attested until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution. 4. Once a gift becomes void after revocation in terms of s. 4 (1) registration of other deeds or registration in the proper folio will not revive a deed that is void",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:348:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1998-51,(1998) 3 Sri LR 46,LKCA,1998,A contract for a building lease ends if the building is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant.,"1. Where a building which is the subject matter is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant, the contract is at an end. 2. The trial Judge on a preponderance of evidence led came to a finding that the plaintiff-appellant had set fire to the house. This is a finding of primary fact by a trial Judge who had seen and heard the witness based upon the credibility of such witness, which is entitled to great weight and utmost consideration. 3. The order of the Rent Board was based on the premise that there existed a building which could be repaired. In the case of a house being let if that is completely burnt, the lease comes to an end, but not where the tenant is able to exercise, many of his rights under the lease notwithstanding the complete destruction of the buildings",SRC029-s145(1),none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:374:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-39-1,commonlii-LKCA-1999-39,(1999) 1 Sri LR 365,LKCA,1999,"A plaintiff has the right to begin a case, but a defendant may begin if they admit the plaintiff's story and contend on a point of law or additional facts.","1. Ordinarily the plaintiff has the right to begin, but where the defendant admits plaintiffs story and contends on some point of law or additional facts to be alleged by him, that the plaintiff is not entitled to any part of the relief, he claims, the defendant has the right to begin. 2. The question as to the party who should begin the case is linked to the question on whom the burden of proof lies in a suit. 3. While S. 101 Evidence Ordinance is concerned with the duty to prove a case as a whole, viz the overall burden of proof S. 103 regulates the burden 366 of proof as to a particular fact, however the devolution of the overall burden is governed by S. 102 which declares that the burden of proof lies on that person who would fail if no such evidence at all were given on either side. When the legal title to the premises is admitted or proved to be in the plaintiff the burden of proof is on the defendant to show he is in lawful possession - defendant must begin the case",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:378:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-50-1,commonlii-LKCA-1999-50,(1999) 1 Sri LR 90,LKCA,1999,A party cannot unilaterally delay the submission of a final account by claiming they have not received necessary information.,"1. The explanation that the final account could not be submitted as the respondents have not been provided with the accounts of the estate of late 'U's mother cannot be accepted because the appellant could have taken proceedings under S. 712 C.P.C. 2. Appellant cannot rely on the provisions of S.108 of the Evidence Ordinance to fix the date of death as being 21. 4. 88 as there is no presumption as to the time or date of a person's death. This is a matter which has to be proved by evidence. 3. The appellant had not controverted the averments in the petition, nor had she specified that the date of the death of 'U' was different to that alleged by the respondents. The estate duty has been assessed on the declaration of the appellant on the basis that the date of death of 'U' was 13. 2. 83. 4. The learned District Judge was justified in making an order directing the appellant to file an additional inventory/final account on the basis that the date of the death of 'U' was 13. 2. 83, and no prejudice has been caused to the appellant",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:563:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-47-1,commonlii-LKCA-2002-47,(2003) 2 Sri LR 144,LKCA,2002,The best possible evidence should be presented in court.,"(i) By calling the notary and the attesting witness the defendants-respon�dents have led the best possible evidence, and that too coming from independent witnesses and in the circumstances there was no neces�sity to call the 2nd defendant-respondent. Per Somawansa, J. ""What section 144 of the Evidence Ordinance provides for is the com�mon sense advice that court may from a proved fact infer another fact which it thinks is likely to be true regard being had to human conduct and common course of natural events."" (ii) The defendants-respondents' position that the deeds were signed by the parties in the presence of the notary and witness is more probable. 145 (iii) Even if in fact the notary has failed to comply with any provision in sec�tion 31 of the Notaries Ordinance, it is well settled law that the validity of the deed is not thereby affected (section 33)",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
