scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:565:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-47-3,commonlii-LKCA-2002-47,(2003) 2 Sri LR 144,LKCA,2002,The validity of a deed is not affected by a notary's failure to comply with section 31 of the Notaries Ordinance.,"(i) By calling the notary and the attesting witness the defendants-respon�dents have led the best possible evidence, and that too coming from independent witnesses and in the circumstances there was no neces�sity to call the 2nd defendant-respondent. Per Somawansa, J. ""What section 144 of the Evidence Ordinance provides for is the com�mon sense advice that court may from a proved fact infer another fact which it thinks is likely to be true regard being had to human conduct and common course of natural events."" (ii) The defendants-respondents' position that the deeds were signed by the parties in the presence of the notary and witness is more probable. 145 (iii) Even if in fact the notary has failed to comply with any provision in sec�tion 31 of the Notaries Ordinance, it is well settled law that the validity of the deed is not thereby affected (section 33)",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:639:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2003-66-1,commonlii-LKCA-2003-66,(2003) 2 Sri LR 341,LKCA,2003,Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an overholding lessee.,"(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",SRC029-s116,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:640:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2003-66-2,commonlii-LKCA-2003-66,(2003) 2 Sri LR 341,LKCA,2003,A plaintiff claiming to be a landlord must establish the existence of privity of contract with the tenant at the relevant date.,"(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",SRC029-s116,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:731:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2006-11-1,commonlii-LKCA-2006-11,(2006) 2 Sri LR 39,LKCA,2006,The onus is on the party to prove their pedigree.,"(1) The onus was on the appellant to prove his pedigree- section 103 Evidence Ordinance, but he had failed to summon any of his predecessors in title or produce any deed or document. (2) Notary's failure to observe his duties with regard to formalities which are not essential to due execution so far as the parties are concemed does not vitiate a deed. (3) The various facts and factors that persuaded the trial Judge not to place any reliance on Deed BV6 are sound. (4) Whether a particular deed is earlier in time and gets priority over another, deed by prior registration under section 7 of the Registration of Documents Ordinance is a mixed question of fact and law-and cannot be raised for the first time in",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:732:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2006-11-2,commonlii-LKCA-2006-11,(2006) 2 Sri LR 39,LKCA,2006,A notary's failure to observe non-essential formalities does not invalidate a deed.,"(1) The onus was on the appellant to prove his pedigree- section 103 Evidence Ordinance, but he had failed to summon any of his predecessors in title or produce any deed or document. (2) Notary's failure to observe his duties with regard to formalities which are not essential to due execution so far as the parties are concemed does not vitiate a deed. (3) The various facts and factors that persuaded the trial Judge not to place any reliance on Deed BV6 are sound. (4) Whether a particular deed is earlier in time and gets priority over another, deed by prior registration under section 7 of the Registration of Documents Ordinance is a mixed question of fact and law-and cannot be raised for the first time in",,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
