ion of advancement in favour of mistress though available in England is not part of Sri Lankan Law. Section 2 of the Trusts Ordinance cannot be utilised to bring .n English Law",,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:902:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1991-33-1,commonlii-LKSC-1991-33,(1991) 1 Sri LR 115,LKSC,1991,The Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property,(i) The Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property. (ii) Extrinsic evidence to prove attendant circumstances can be properly be received in evidence to prove a resulting trust,,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:903:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1991-33-2,commonlii-LKSC-1991-33,(1991) 1 Sri LR 115,LKSC,1991,Extrinsic evidence to prove attendant circumstances can be properly be received in evidence to prove a resulting trust,(i) The Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property. (ii) Extrinsic evidence to prove attendant circumstances can be properly be received in evidence to prove a resulting trust,,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:957:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1998-18-1,commonlii-LKSC-1998-18,(1998) 3 Sri LR 227,LKSC,1998,"1. The burden of proof is on the defendant to show that he is in lawful possession of the premises. The defendant failed to discharge the burden; hence the plaintiff was entitled to judgment. 2. Where the evidence is inadequate, the District Court is not required to ensure the production of relevant evidence before giving judgment Per G. P. S. de Silva, CJ. 'Neither section 134 of the Civil Procedure Code nor section 165 of the Evidence Ordinance was meant to fill in the gaps in the presentation of its case by a party to the action. While these sections confer a power upon the court, they do not place a burden upon the court","1. The burden of proof is on the defendant to show that he is in lawful possession of the premises. The defendant failed to discharge the burden; hence the plaintiff was entitled to judgment. 2. Where the evidence is inadequate, the District Court is not required to ensure the production of relevant evidence before giving judgment Per G. P. S. de Silva, CJ. 'Neither section 134 of the Civil Procedure Code nor section 165 of the Evidence Ordinance was meant to fill in the gaps in the presentation of its case by a party to the action. While these sections confer a power upon the court, they do not place a burden upon the court",,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:976:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1999-41-1,commonlii-LKSC-1999-41,(1999) 2 Sri LR 152,LKSC,1999,"1. The question of due execution of the deed of revocation did not arise at the trial. In any event at the time of the trial the Notary was dead and the execution of the deed was proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by calling Gafoor. 2. There was no issue raised at the trial on the question of the 1st defendant's consent to the deed of revocation. In any event the evidence of the plaintiff and Gafoor as well as a document signed by the 1st defendant before the Assistant Commissioner of Agrarian Services agreeing to enter the plaintiff's name in the paddy lands register as the owner of the land in dispute established her consent to the revocation. 3. The failure of the Notary to observe the provisions of section 31 of the Notaries Ordinance in executing the deed of revocation did not make it invalid; for in terms of section 33 of the Ordinance, the deed shall not be deemed to be invalid by reason only of such failure. 4. The evidence established that the deed of revocation was executed on 9.6.1969. Per Dheeraratne, J. ""It was quite clear that words and figures indicating nine in the protocol had been altered to read as nineteen. The alterations were not in the handwriting of Abdul Gafoor""","1. The question of due execution of the deed of revocation did not arise at the trial. In any event at the time of the trial the Notary was dead and the execution of the deed was proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by calling Gafoor. 2. There was no issue raised at the trial on the question of the 1st defendant's consent to the deed of revocation. In any event the evidence of the plaintiff and Gafoor as well as a document signed by the 1st defendant before the Assistant Commissioner of Agrarian Services agreeing to enter the plaintiff's name in the paddy lands register as the owner of the land in dispute established her consent to the revocation. 3. The failure of the Notary to observe the provisions of section 31 of the Notaries Ordinance in executing the deed of revocation did not make it invalid; for in terms of section 33 of the Ordinance, the deed shall not be deemed to be invalid by reason only of such failure. 4. The evidence established that the deed of revocation was executed on 9.6.1969. Per Dheeraratne, J. ""It was quite clear that words and figures indicating nine in the protocol had been altered to read as nineteen. The alterations were not in the handwriting of Abdul Gafoor""",,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1064:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-2009-8-1,commonlii-LKSC-2009-8,(2009) 1 Sri LR 232,LKSC,2009,"In determining the condition of the land in 1968 and the condition of the land at the time of acquisition for the purpose of computing compensation to be payable, the Board of Review had arrived at a decision, not purely on facts, but on an inference on the applicability of the Act No. 15 of 1968 and section 7 of the Land Acquisition Act","(1) In determining the condition of the land in 1968 and the condition of the land at the time of acquisition for the purpose of computing compensation to be payable, the Board of Review had arrived at a decision, not purely on facts, but on an inference on the applicability of the Act No. 15 of 1968 and section 7 of the Land Acquisition Act. (2) When an acquisition of land falls within the purview of section 4(2) of the Colombo District (Low Lying Areas) Reclamation and Development Board Act No. 15 of 1968, the valuation of the property has to be computed on the basis ofthe valuation as at the date of the commencement of the Act No. 15 of 1968. (3) When it is claimed that the condition of the land in question had changed from its original position at the time of its acquisition, the person who makes that statement should lead evidence to prove that position in terms of section 102 of the Evidence Ordinance It would be presumed that in the ordinary course of nature there was no change in the condition of the land",,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1065:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-2009-8-2,commonlii-LKSC-2009-8,(2009) 1 Sri LR 232,LKSC,2009,When an acquisition of land falls within the purview of section 4,"(1) In determining the condition of the land in 1968 and the condition of the land at the time of acquisition for the purpose of computing compensation to be payable, the Board of Review had arrived at a decision, not purely on facts, but on an inference on the applicability of the Act No. 15 of 1968 and section 7 of the Land Acquisition Act. (2) When an acquisition of land falls within the purview of section 4(2) of the Colombo District (Low Lying Areas) Reclamation and Development Board Act No. 15 of 1968, the valuation of the property has to be computed on the basis ofthe valuation as at the date of the commencement of the Act No. 15 of 1968. (3) When it is claimed that the condition of the land in question had changed from its original position at the time of its acquisition, the person who makes that statement should lead evidence to prove that position in terms of section 102 of the Evidence Ordinance It would be presumed that in the ordinary course of nature there was no change in the condition of the land",,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1066:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-2009-8-3,commonlii-LKSC-2009-8,(2009) 1 Sri LR 232,LKSC,2009,"of the Colombo District (Low Lying Areas) Reclamation and Development Board Act No. 15 of 1968, the valuation of the property has to be computed on the basis ofthe valuation as at the date of the commencement of the Act No. 15 of 1968","(1) In determining the condition of the land in 1968 and the condition of the land at the time of acquisition for the purpose of computing compensation to be payable, the Board of Review had arrived at a decision, not purely on facts, but on an inference on the applicability of the Act No. 15 of 1968 and section 7 of the Land Acquisition Act. (2) When an acquisition of land falls within the purview of section 4(2) of the Colombo District (Low Lying Areas) Reclamation and Development Board Act No. 15 of 1968, the valuation of the property has to be computed on the basis ofthe valuation as at the date of the commencement of the Act No. 15 of 1968. (3) When it is claimed that the condition of the land in question had changed from its original position at the time of its acquisition, the person who makes that statement should lead evidence to prove that position in terms of section 102 of the Evidence Ordinance It would be presumed that in the ordinary course of nature there was no change in the condition of the land",,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1072:draftly-rule-courts-coa-15.-ca-dcf-1058_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-15.-ca-dcf-1058_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A conditional transfer of immovable property is not considered a mortgage for the purposes of the Debt Conciliation Ordinance, and therefore, Section 43 of the Ordinance does not apply to such transfers.",The amendment to the definition of ‘mortgage’ made by Act No. 5 of 1959 has not altered the law relating to the creation of a mortgage over immovable property and has not recognized as a mode of creating a mortgage the execution of a conditional transfer.,SRC029,ok,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1087:draftly-rule-courts-coa-BOR-0001-2027.03.2026-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-BOR-0001-2027.03.2026-1.pdf,,COA,2026,"The valuation submitted by the Appellant's expert witness must be supported by evidence, both oral and documentary.","Hence, it clearly, appears that the valuation submitted by Mr. Kaleel- Private Valuer for the original Appellant, of the land intended to be acquired by the Respondent on the date of the publication of the notice under section 7 of the Act in the Gazette (A2/R7) on 03.01.1994 had not been supported by any evidence oral or documentary by the Appellant and therefore, it is without any basis and as such it cannot, sustain both in fact and law and therefore, it should be rejected as rightly, held by LABR.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1088:draftly-rule-courts-coa-BOR-0001-2320.02.2026.pdf-1,courts-coa-BOR-0001-2320.02.2026.pdf,,COA,2026,"The burden of proof lies on the appellants to prove what they had asserted before it in terms of section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance to be read with sections 101,102 and 103 thereof.","rden of proof will lie on the Appellants to prove what they had asserted before it in terms of section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance to be read with sections 101,102 and 103 thereof.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1098:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,The burden of proof lies on the plaintiff to show that the execution of the deed is tainted with fraud.,"When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",SRC029-s101,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1125:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,"The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.","The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.",SRC029-s110,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1132:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A court can only investigate title in a partition action within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral.","Although there is a duty cast on the court to investigate title in a partition action, the court can do so only within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral. The court cannot go on a voyage of discovery tracing the title and finding the shares in the corpus for them [Thilagaratnam v. Athpunathan and Others (1996) 2 Sri.L.R. 66].",SRC029-s90,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1150:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf,,COA,,A photocopy of an original document is admissible as evidence.,"Secondary evidences such as photocopies of the originals are admissible under Section 65, 66 of the Evidence Ordinance.",,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1215:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-Writ-742-24-final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-Writ-742-24-final.pdf,,COA,,The Board shall consider all circumstances of the case when deciding whether a transfer of immovable property is in reality a mortgage.,"21A. (1) In any proceedings under this Ordinance in regard to an application relating to a transfer or conditional transfer of immovable property, the Board shall, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance (Chapter 70), or sections 91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance (Chapter 14) and for the purpose of deciding whether or not such transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage, take into consideration all the circumstances of the case and in particular the following matters:-",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1248:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf,,COA,,A person who asserts the existence of a fact has the burden of proof.,lithe burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1285:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_764_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_764_99.pdf,,COA,,"A lessee or licensee will not be permitted to question the title of the person who gave them the lease or license, or set up a want of title in that person.","Whether it is a licensee or lessee, the question of title is foreign to a suit in ejectment against either, the licensee obtaining possession is deemed to obtain it upon the terms that he will not dispute the title of the Plaintiff without whose permission he would not have got it.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1287:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_788_97.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_788_97.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof shifts to the defendant to show independent rights in the form of prescription once the plaintiff's paper title is undisputed.,Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them,SRC029-s68,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1289:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_823_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_823_98.pdf,,COA,,A party's admission of a fact in a proceeding need not be proved.,Section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance states that what is admitted by the parties need not be proved.,SRC029-s58,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1298:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,A contract may be avoided by the court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1344:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_842_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_842_99.pdf,,COA,,A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfilment of his contractual obligations.,Indeed a lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfilment of his contractual obligation.,SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1355:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_619_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_619_99.pdf,,COA,,A party cannot deny an admission they made in a lease agreement.,"The contention of the learned President's Counsel on this matter, in my opinion is not without merit.",SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1362:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_hc_hambantota_33_2008.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_hc_hambantota_33_2008.pdf,,COA,2008,The law does not afford concessions to the conduct of persons who retain their self-control and give effect to a murderous intention in a more scheming and calculated manner.,"The law does not afford concessions to the conduct of such persons, who are nevertheless the direct fallout from grave and continuing cumulative provocation.",SRC029-s105,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1399:draftly-rule-courts-coa-HCC-21-20-JUDGMENT-4.pdf-1,courts-coa-HCC-21-20-JUDGMENT-4.pdf,,COA,,"A witness's credibility is not affected by trivial contradictions in their testimony, especially when they are consistent on the main points of the incident.","I hold that this trivial contradiction does not affect the credibility of this witness. On the other hand, he will remember what is important, which should have always been in his memory, because that incident would not fade from his memory.",SRC029-s165,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1440:draftly-rule-courts-coa-WRT-0054-20.pdf-1,courts-coa-WRT-0054-20.pdf,,COA,,The jurisdiction of the Court under Article 140 is supervisory in nature and primarily concerned with the legality of administrative action rather than the adjudication of disputed private rights.,It is well settled that the jurisdiction of this Court under Article 140 is supervisory in nature and is primarily concerned with the legality of administrative action rather than the adjudication of disputed private rights.,SRC029-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1477:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,No issue was raised before the District Court as to the identity of the corpus.,It is observed that no issue was raised before the District Court as to the identity of the corpus.,SRC029-s23,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1485:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A deed must be proved in accordance with the requirements of the Evidence Ordinance and the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance to constitute a valid basis for resisting restoration of possession.,"In these circumstances the Appellant failed to establish that the purported deed constituted a valid instrument capable of transferring title. Consequently, the Appellant failed to demonstrate that he derived title from a person who obtained rights under the final decree.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1487:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party who claims prescriptive title to a particular corpus must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus.,"A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1497:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf,,SC,2022,A mere presence of a prayer for a declaration of title cannot make the action a rei vindicatio action.,Mere presence of a prayer for a declaration of title cannot make the action a re vidicatio action.,SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1520:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_117_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_117_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"Parole evidence is permitted to prove a constructive trust between parties, despite Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance.","In order to prove a constructive trust, is parole evidence permitted in view of the principles set out in Section 2 of the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance? This question was discussed in the case of Dayawathi and Others Vs Gunasekara and Another [1991] 1 SLR 115.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1535:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Declarations regarding relationship found in documents are relevant and admissible under sections 32(5) and 32(6) of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It is under these provisions, to wit, section 32(5) and (6) and section 50 of the Evidence Ordinance that it is possible to admit evidence, otherwise hearsay, of deceased persons figuring in a genealogical tree such as one often comes across in a partition case.",SRC029-s32,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1566:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proof lies on the party asserting that the condition of the land has changed.,"When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",SRC029-s101,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1582:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"The burden of proof in terms of sec 101 and 102 of the Evidence Ordinance rests with the Respondent to this case to prove that the said Deed 5414 is fraudulent, which the Respondent has failed to discharge.","When considering the above facts, the question of whether the High Court of Civil Appeal failed to consider that the burden of proof in terms of sec 101 and 102 of the Evidence Ordinance rests with the Respondent to this case to prove that the said Deed 5414 is fraudulent, which the Respondent has failed to discharge could be answered in affirmative.",,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1621:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the party who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,"In terms of Section 103, since the Appellant has raised issues disputing that P 1 and P 2 is forgery he is the person who wishes the court to believe the existence of that fact. Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.",SRC029-s103,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1647:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.",This evidence contained in the affidavit has not been challenged either by raising an issue on this matter or calling the Fiscal Officer who executed the writ and eliciting the fact that possession had not been handed over as claimed by the Appellant. No independent evidence was led to rebut this presumption.,SRC029-s114(d),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1651:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_160_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_160_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"Parole evidence can be used to prove a constructive trust, even if the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.",The Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Sec. 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1686:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,"I am in respectful agreement with a view expressed by His Lordship Justice Dheeraratne. After considering the above legal literature, I would like to follow the principle laid down in the case of Dayawathi and Others Vs. Gunasekera and Another (Supra) and I hold that Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not operate as a bar to lead parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and to prove that the transferor did not intend to dispose of beneficial interest in the property.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1689:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1691:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A party cannot adduce oral evidence against the contents of a notarially executed deed to prove fraud and/or duress/intimidation, unless the fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.","Under section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance oral evidence could not be led which is inconsistent with the contents of the deed. Under proviso (1) to section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance any fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1732:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The probative value of a death certificate is limited to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers.,The probative value of the contents of a death certificate issued under both Ordinance No. 1 of 1895 and Births and Deaths Registration Act No. 17 of 1951 is limited in terms of those two enactments to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers and applies to.,SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1741:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A document marked 'P3' without objection at the closure of the case is deemed to be admitted as evidence.,"Therefore, at this juncture, the document marked ‘P3’ ought to be admitted as evidence for all purposes of law considering the fact that no objection was taken at the closure of the Plaintiffs-Appellants case.",SRC029-s114,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1753:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_211_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_211_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,The admissibility of a document in a partition action is governed by both Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance and Section 90 of that Ordinance.,"In view of these applicable statutory provisions, it was further contended by the learned Counsel that the error committed by the trial Court, in its failure to consider that vital factor, which in turn resulted in the dismissal of the Plaint, had been cured by the appellate Court by acting on the said presumption.",,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1758:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Formal admissions recorded at the trial need no further proof unless the Court wants them to be proved.,"58. No fact need be proved in any proceeding which the parties thereto or their agents agree to admit at the hearing, or which, before the hearing, they agree to admit by any writing under their hands, or which by any rule of pleading in force at the time they are deemed to have admitted by their pleadings: Provided that the court may, in its discretion, require the facts admitted to be proved otherwise than by such admissions.",SRC029-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1764:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A lessee or licensee cannot question the title of the person who gave them the lease or licence, and must first quit the land before challenging the title.","No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property; and no person who came upon any immovable property by the licence of the person in possession thereof shall be permitted to deny that such person had a title to such possession at the time when such licence was given.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1769:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.","A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1771:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or their heirs on the basis of the period they possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1821:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1831:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person who strikes another with a formidable weapon must be presumed to have known that the act is imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes.,Thus a man who strikes at the back of another a violent blow with a formidable weapon must be taken to know that he is doing an act imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes and that a probable result of his act will be to cause that person’s death.,SRC029-s6,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1836:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"If no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.","Hence, if no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.",SRC029-s154,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1867:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit.,"A licencee as the Defendant who entered the property in dispute with the leave and licence of Plaintiff cannot in law challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit, in this case the Plaintiff party.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1908:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A contract requires an offer and acceptance.,"every contract requires an offer and acceptance. Therefore an offer or promise which is not accepted, is not actionable [vide Justice Weerasooriya in Muthukuda v. Sumanatwathie, (1962) 65 NLR 205, 208, 209].",SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1910:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf,,SC,2020,Courts may countenance infirmities in proving original ownership of land if it is a realistic approach rather than a legalistic one.,"Courts by and large countenance infirmities in this regard, if infirmities they be, in an approach which is realistic rather than legalistic, as to do otherwise would be to put the relief given by partition decrees outside the reach of very many persons seeking to end the co-ownership.",SRC029-s32(5),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1929:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A judge must consider all prayers in a plaint, even in an ex parte trial.","Even in an ex parte trial, the judge must act according to law and ensure that the relief claimed is due in fact and in law, and must dismiss the plaintiff's claim if he is not entitled to it. An ex parte judgment cannot be entered without a hearing and an adjudication.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1931:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A party must prove the due execution of a document to establish its validity.,"The learned judge has noted that both the 2nd Defendant and the Plaintiff have not discharged the burden cast on them by section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance read with section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, which required them to prove that the two documents",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1946:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A court must consider the relevance of evidence before admitting it.,"Evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of the existence or non-existence of every fact in issue, and of such other facts as are hereinafter declared to be relevant and of no others.",SRC029-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1950:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1955:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.,"The judicial approach to authenticity was clearly articulated in the case of Robins v Grogan [43 N.L.R. 269 at 270], where the Court held that a document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.",SRC029-s67,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1984:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2017:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,The burden of proof lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.,Section 102 – “The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.”,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2048:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A tenant cannot deny the landlord's title to the property during the continuance of the tenancy.,"No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property;",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2059:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,Uncontradicted evidence adduced by a party is a fact in favour of that party's case.,"In other words, uncontradicted evidence adduced by the 2nd Defendant in the trial is a fact in favour of the 2nd Defendant’s case.",SRC029-s03,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2067:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A document cannot be produced in evidence without leave in terms of section 175(2) of the Civil Procedure Code if it was not included in a list filed in terms of section 121(2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,It must be noted here that a document cannot be produced in evidence without leave in terms of section 175(2) of the Civil Procedure Code if it was not included in a list filed in terms of section 121(2) of the Civil Procedure Code and that new matters cannot be introduced during re-examination without permission in terms of section 138(3) of the Evidence Ordinance.,,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2077:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A notary who attests a document in terms of Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is generally competent to testify under section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance.,A notary who attests a deed is an attesting witness within the meaning of that expression in section 68 and section 69 of the Evidence Ordinance.,SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2088:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party may lead evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2097:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.","A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.",SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2125:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A document required by law to be attested must have at least one attesting witness called to prove its execution.,"According to that section, such document shall not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there is an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2156:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.,"Moreover, a public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.",SRC029-s114(f),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-002.json:167:  "supporting_quote": "That a power of attorney which is ex-facie duly executed in India attracts the presumption laid down in section 85 of the Evidence Ordinance that is was so duly executed and authenticated. Held further : That according to the provisions of the Indian Law applicable, a Sub-Registrar is a person duly authorised by law to execute and authenticate a document such as a Power of Attorney and therefore the document in the present case is ex facie duly executed",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-002.json:207:  "supporting_quote": "That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. \"The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous\". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-002.json:208:  "statute_section": "SRC029-s95",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-006.json:157:  "supporting_quote": "Apart from the fact that Ss.91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not permit the receipt of evidence to vary the terms of a notarially executed deed so as to superimpose on a simple transfer deed characteristics such as mortgages or agreements to retransfer yet even on the facts no trust can be held to have been established. Time was of the essence of the alleged oral agreement and the constructive trust yet there was no evidence that the money was even tendered in time",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-004.json:167:  "supporting_quote": "(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-004.json:177:  "supporting_quote": "(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-004.json:187:  "supporting_quote": "(1) The Notary's evidence which was accepted by the trial Court establishes the due execution of the impugned deed of gift. As the original and duplicate of the deed were not available, secondary evidence including the protocol was admissible under s. 65 of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) In regard to the registration of the impugned deed of gift although the registration entries and several other relevant documents in the Land Registry were missing still particulars in the Day Book maintained under the Registration of Documents Ordinance by the Land Registry of deeds tendered for registration and deeds registered and the entry of the grantee's name as owner in the Index are admissible as secondary evidence in proof of the contents of the lost or missing folio in the continuation of which the 1st defendant's own deed was registered and of the fact of registration of the deed P1 in the missing folio. (3) Accordingly the deed of gift relied on by the plaintiff prevails over the deed relied on by the 1st defendant both by priority of execution and priority of registration. 250 (4) Although lack of acceptance of the deed of gift was pleaded in the answer no issue on it was raised at the trial There was acceptance on the face of the deed. The question that acceptance has not been proved was raised when written submissions were filed but this is too belated to be entertained Quaere : Is the protocol of a deed kept by the attesting Notary an original document in terms of the Evidence Ordinance ?",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-003.json:7:  "supporting_quote": "The learned District Judge had misdirected himself in holding that the averments in the plaint constituted several causes of action which were embodied in one action. The plaint refers to one cause of action against all the defendant respondents though various incidents which had taken place between 1955 and 1963 were mentioned. On the facts pleaded and proved in the case there was no misjoinder of parties and causes of action. The title deed was properly proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. There was no issue as to whether the notary complied with the provisions of section 31(9) of the Notaries Ordinance and no questions had been asked from him regarding that when he gave evidence",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-003.json:17:  "supporting_quote": "The learned District Judge had misdirected himself in holding that the averments in the plaint constituted several causes of action which were embodied in one action. The plaint refers to one cause of action against all the defendant respondents though various incidents which had taken place between 1955 and 1963 were mentioned. On the facts pleaded and proved in the case there was no misjoinder of parties and causes of action. The title deed was properly proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance. There was no issue as to whether the notary complied with the provisions of section 31(9) of the Notaries Ordinance and no questions had been asked from him regarding that when he gave evidence",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-003.json:57:  "supporting_quote": "P4 is a more solemn document than D3 where the declarant was bound by statute to declare the truth. Sections 32(5), 32(6) and 50 of the Evidence Ordinance refer to relevance and admissibility and not to presumptions. But such evidence can always be countered by other evidence, as in this case, by evidence more convincing and cogent",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-003.json:66:  "statement": "The Evidence Ordinance sections 48 and 50 refer to relevance and admissibility, not presumptions.",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-003.json:67:  "supporting_quote": "P4 is a more solemn document than D3 where the declarant was bound by statute to declare the truth. Sections 32(5), 32(6) and 50 of the Evidence Ordinance refer to relevance and admissibility and not to presumptions. But such evidence can always be countered by other evidence, as in this case, by evidence more convincing and cogent",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-003.json:137:  "supporting_quote": "The oral agreement by the defendant appellant to reconvey the lands to the plaintiff -respondent on payment of Rs.3000/- does not give rise to a trust. Further facts, clearly indicative of a trust must be proved before a trust can arise. The facts that adequate consideration did not pass, that plaintiff -respondent remained in possession after execution of the conveyance and that an oral promise was made to re-transfer are insufficient. Further this oral agreement relied on by the plaintiff -respondent amounts to a contract for the transfer of immovable property which is invalid and cannot be enforced as it contravenes section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance. Though parol evidence of this oral agreement was admitted at the trial without objections it would still be prohibited by section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-021.json:7:  "supporting_quote": "(i) By calling the notary and the attesting witness the defendants-respon'dents have led the best possible evidence, and that too coming from independent witnesses and in the circumstances there was no neces'sity to call the 2nd defendant-respondent. Per Somawansa, J. \"What section 144 of the Evidence Ordinance provides for is the com'mon sense advice that court may from a proved fact infer another fact which it thinks is likely to be true regard being had to human conduct and common course of natural events.\" (ii) The defendants-respondents' position that the deeds were signed by the parties in the presence of the notary and witness is more probable. 145 (iii) Even if in fact the notary has failed to comply with any provision in sec'tion 31 of the Notaries Ordinance, it is well settled law that the validity of the deed is not thereby affected (section 33)",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-021.json:17:  "supporting_quote": "(i) By calling the notary and the attesting witness the defendants-respon'dents have led the best possible evidence, and that too coming from independent witnesses and in the circumstances there was no neces'sity to call the 2nd defendant-respondent. Per Somawansa, J. \"What section 144 of the Evidence Ordinance provides for is the com'mon sense advice that court may from a proved fact infer another fact which it thinks is likely to be true regard being had to human conduct and common course of natural events.\" (ii) The defendants-respondents' position that the deeds were signed by the parties in the presence of the notary and witness is more probable. 145 (iii) Even if in fact the notary has failed to comply with any provision in sec'tion 31 of the Notaries Ordinance, it is well settled law that the validity of the deed is not thereby affected (section 33)",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-023.json:227:  "supporting_quote": "(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-023.json:228:  "statute_section": "SRC029-s116",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-023.json:237:  "supporting_quote": "(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-023.json:238:  "statute_section": "SRC029-s116",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-014.json:17:  "supporting_quote": "1. The explanation that the final account could not be submitted as the respondents have not been provided with the accounts of the estate of late 'U's mother cannot be accepted because the appellant could have taken proceedings under S. 712 C.P.C. 2. Appellant cannot rely on the provisions of S.108 of the Evidence Ordinance to fix the date of death as being 21. 4. 88 as there is no presumption as to the time or date of a person's death. This is a matter which has to be proved by evidence. 3. The appellant had not controverted the averments in the petition, nor had she specified that the date of the death of 'U' was different to that alleged by the respondents. The estate duty has been assessed on the declaration of the appellant on the basis that the date of death of 'U' was 13. 2. 83. 4. The learned District Judge was justified in making an order directing the appellant to file an additional inventory/final account on the basis that the date of the death of 'U' was 13. 2. 83, and no prejudice has been caused to the appellant",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-013.json:227:  "supporting_quote": "1. Ordinarily the plaintiff has the right to begin, but where the defendant admits plaintiffs story and contends on some point of law or additional facts to be alleged by him, that the plaintiff is not entitled to any part of the relief, he claims, the defendant has the right to begin. 2. The question as to the party who should begin the case is linked to the question on whom the burden of proof lies in a suit. 3. While S. 101 Evidence Ordinance is concerned with the duty to prove a case as a whole, viz the overall burden of proof S. 103 regulates the burden 366 of proof as to a particular fact, however the devolution of the overall burden is governed by S. 102 which declares that the burden of proof lies on that person who would fail if no such evidence at all were given on either side. When the legal title to the premises is admitted or proved to be in the plaintiff the burden of proof is on the defendant to show he is in lawful possession - defendant must begin the case",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-030.json:77:  "supporting_quote": "that the prima facie evidence of the declarations made under Section 41 of the Buddhist Temporalities Ordinance was rebutted by the oral evidence and documentary evidence; that section 13 of Evidence Ordinance enabled Appellant to lead in evidence a document in handwriting of the Tutor admitted under section 32(7) of Evidence Ordinance to deny the right claimed by the Respondent. 274",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-030.json:86:  "statement": "The Evidence Ordinance can be used to deny a claimed right.",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-030.json:87:  "supporting_quote": "that the prima facie evidence of the declarations made under Section 41 of the Buddhist Temporalities Ordinance was rebutted by the oral evidence and documentary evidence; that section 13 of Evidence Ordinance enabled Appellant to lead in evidence a document in handwriting of the Tutor admitted under section 32(7) of Evidence Ordinance to deny the right claimed by the Respondent. 274",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-030.json:197:  "supporting_quote": "(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\judge\pack-030.json:198:  "statute_section": "SRC029-s114",
